**Record (Vendored C provenance)** · `rec:crypto:vendored-c-provenance` The Rust bindings and elements package reuse the C snapshots of (`amend:crypto:binding-boundary`): secp256k1-sys carries `1ad5185cd42c0636104129fcc9f6a4bf9c67cc40`, and secp256k1-zkp-sys carries `6152622613fdf1c5af6f31f74c427c4e9ee120ce`. Their bundled C COPYING files retain the MIT permission text; upstream auxiliary files retain their own notices. The higher-level packages, including bitcoin, carry no independent C snapshot. The enabled features and module set remain those of (`amend:crypto:wallet-codec-features`) and (`amend:crypto:wallet-artifact-binding`). The ownership patches change no bundled C source, resolved feature, dependency or module selection.

**Amendment (Owned secret carriers)** · `amend:crypto:owned-secret-carriers` The declared patches remove Copy from SecretKey, Keypair, SharedSecret and ElligatorSwiftSharedSecret, and replace their diagnostic formatting with constants independent of their values. The DisplaySecret carrier and display_secret helpers are removed. PrivateKey and Xpriv have no Display implementation: fmt_wif, to_wif, encode and to_base58 remain explicitly named secret exports, not implicit formatting. No first-party production caller uses a removed display path; the carrier controls use only constant Debug. Each binding carrier calls the existing non_secure_erase operation on drop; no new unsafe operation is introduced. PrivateKey, Xpriv and both TweakedKeypair wrappers also lose Copy and have constant Debug, including Xpriv's chain-code and derivation metadata. Their binding fields erase on drop, and Xpriv separately erases its chain code with the existing safe erasure wrapper. BIP-32 ckd_priv and Elements value-factor addition borrow a SecretKey through add_secret_tweak, retaining the existing scalar-addition operation without converting the secret to the public Scalar carrier. The two whitelist test sites use the same borrowed operation and explicit owned clones. Public Scalar remains Copy and must not carry secrets; its explicit conversion API is not an admitted secret route. Returned WIF and extended-key decode buffers have erasing drop guards on success and every subsequent error. Factor parsing decodes directly into a guarded fixed array, including invalid-character and out-of-range errors. The base58 decoder itself is an unpatched dependency: allocations discarded inside decode_check before it returns, including checksum failures, are outside these guards. Fixing those internal paths needs a separately reviewed decoder change; this patch does not claim to erase them. Explicit owned Clone and explicit byte export remain available. Rust-level constructor copies into binding storage, parser iterator bytes, HMAC outputs in ckd_priv, and explicit export buffers remain bounded or caller-owned temporaries, not a guarantee of a single physical copy. Vec growth and map growth can move owners and leave prior allocation bytes; Drop on the final owner does not erase those prior locations. SecretTweak, SharedSecret, ElligatorSwiftSharedSecret and factor comparisons and hashing, and the factor zero branches, are variable-time; Xpriv also compares its ordinary chain-code and derivation fields. SecretKey equality uses the upstream full-length XOR accumulator with a volatile read, while Keypair and its tweaked wrappers compare and hash their derived public key. No general constant-time comparison guarantee is claimed for the carriers. The binding SecretKey and Keypair FromStr paths also retain their upstream hexadecimal staging buffers; the planned wrapper excludes those paths rather than claiming that this patch erased them. Owned-carrier erasure does not erase the C library's internal temporaries or the binding's algorithm buffers; a single-home wrapper would not by itself erase them either. In vendor/elements/src/blind.rs, TxOut::unblind now transfers the rewound opening.message into a private erasing guard before any fallible message parsing, and borrows its message array instead of copying it into the parser. RangeProofMessage::from_byte_array retains its public by-value interface but immediately guards the received array and delegates to the same borrowed parser. The guards erase initialized message bytes through the binding's existing volatile-write primitive on success and every subsequent error before releasing their storage. The guarded opening.message is a boxed slice. The stack buffers and copies in secp256k1-zkp::RangeProof::rewind before it returns that box, caller-owned arrays passed by value, move temporaries and the existing factor-constructor copies in RangeProofMessage::from_bytes are not erased by these guards; covering those would require separate caller or upstream buffer-lifecycle changes. This is a source-level Drop property, not an observation of reclaimed memory or a claim that the guards remove every physical copy. Compiler-created transient copies and process snapshots are outside this patch: process snapshots include core dumps, swap, hibernation and same-user reads of process memory. The planned secret-owning wrapper must address allocation movement, locking, dump and fork controls, and must never route a secret through Scalar, FromStr or Display under (`rule:crypto:secret-integration`). (`open:crypto:dependency-admission`) stays open for that wrapper and the signing operation evidence. Secret serde remains unselected by the unchanged feature declaration. The safe trait-ambiguity and diagnostic controls in the pn-wallet::decoder::owned::owned_secrets test detect implicit-copy, implicit-display or diagnostic regressions for the named carriers; erasure on drop is a property of the patched source, not an observation of reclaimed memory.

**Record (Public scalars and secret blindings)** · `rec:crypto:public-scalars-and-secret-blindings` Public Tweak remains Copy with its public encoding. Secret blinding uses instead own SecretTweak, which is non-Copy, has constant Debug and erases through the existing safe erasure wrapper on drop. Generator, rangeproof, Pedersen and surjection operations accept this secret carrier; Elements asset and value BlindingFactor own it, and their diagnostics are constant. TxOutSecrets and SurjectionInput consequently lose Copy. Borrowed construction retains each returned ephemeral or issuance key's owner. Explicit clones create independent drop-bearing owners where a borrowed API must return a new owner or where the adaptive scalar calculation needs mutable scratch storage. AssetBlindingNonce and the PSET global scalars are public disclosures and retain their public representation: conversion from a secret factor is explicit and preserves the existing published encoding. This distinction does not make an arbitrary Tweak containing secret bytes safe; callers must select the owned carrier for secret use. The two whitelist test call sites are migrated to the owned operation without changing their assertions; other upstream test modules and examples remain unchanged. No upstream test-suite execution is claimed. The detached wallet fuzz workspace routes the same declared vendor packages through its own patch table, so its decoder campaigns build the patched carriers of the named source tree rather than registry substitutes.
